          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Belt vs Other Sanders</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A belt sander removes material faster than any other sander, which makes it the right tool for flattening and stripping and the wrong tool for finishing. It will also take a gouge out of a workpiece in about two seconds if you set it down carelessly, and most of what separates a good machine from a poor one is how hard it makes that mistake to commit.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: What a Belt Sander Is Actually For -->
        <div id="what-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Belt Sander Is Actually For
          </h2>

          <p>
            A belt sander is a stock removal tool. Treating it as a sander in the general sense, interchangeable with an orbital, is where most disappointment and most gouged workpieces originate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Removes Material Fast</h3>

          <p>
            The abrasive travels in one continuous direction at high speed under the weight of the machine, which removes wood at a rate no orbital or random orbit sander approaches. That is the point of the tool and the reason it is dangerous to a workpiece.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flattening Is Its Best Use</h3>

          <p>
            Levelling a glued-up panel, taking down a proud joint, or flattening a rough sawn board are jobs where nothing else in a home workshop is as effective. The flat platen references against the high points and removes them first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stripping Is Its Second Best Use</h3>

          <p>
            Paint, varnish and old finish come off far faster than with any other sander, particularly with a coarse belt at a lower speed to avoid melting the finish into the abrasive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Is Not a Finishing Tool</h3>

          <p>
            A belt runs in one direction and leaves scratches along that direction, which show clearly under finish. A belt sander gets a surface flat and rough; a random orbit sander then makes it smooth. Skipping the second stage is visible on the finished piece.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Will Gouge in Under Two Seconds</h3>

          <p>
            The machine must be moving before it touches the work and must stay moving until it leaves. A stationary running belt digs a hollow almost instantly, and correcting that hollow means taking the whole surface down to meet it.
          </p>

