        <!-- SECTION 2: Belt Sizes and Which to Choose -->
        <div id="sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Belt Sizes and Which to Choose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Belt sanders are described by belt width and length, and the size determines what the machine is good at far more than the brand does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Three by twenty-one is the sensible default.</strong> It balances coverage against controllability, weighs little enough for extended use, and belts are available everywhere in every grit. For most workshops this is the machine to own.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Four by twenty-four covers ground much faster.</strong> The extra width and length remove material considerably quicker on large flat surfaces, at the cost of significant weight and much less forgiving handling. This is a benchtop and floor machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Three by eighteen is lighter and cheaper.</strong> Slightly less coverage and a shorter platen, which makes it marginally less good at flattening but easier to handle. A reasonable choice for occasional use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Compact machines under three inches are specialists.</strong> They exist to reach places full size sanders cannot, and they are excellent at that and poor at everything else. Buy one as a second sander, not a first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Platen length affects flatness more than width.</strong> A longer platen bridges across dips and removes the high spots, which is exactly what flattening requires. A short machine follows the existing contour rather than correcting it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check belt availability before buying.</strong> Unusual sizes mean ordering online every time and paying more. The common sizes are stocked in every hardware store in a full range of grits, which matters when a belt tears mid-job.
          </p>
        </div>

