        <!-- SECTION 4: Technique That Prevents Gouging -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Technique That Prevents Gouging
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every belt sander mistake produces the same result: a dish in the workpiece that then has to be sanded out across the entire surface. The technique that avoids it is specific and easy to learn.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start the machine off the work.</strong> Bring it up to speed in the air, lower it onto the surface while already moving forward, and lift it off while still moving. Touching down a stationary belt is the classic way to dig a hollow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the weight of the machine do the work.</strong> A belt sander is heavy for a reason. Adding downward pressure does not increase removal proportionally, it increases heat, clogs the belt, and makes the machine harder to steer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep it flat and keep it moving.</strong> Tilting concentrates the pressure on one edge of the belt, which cuts a groove. Pausing anywhere cuts a hollow. Overlapping passes at a steady walking pace produces an even surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sand with the grain, always.</strong> Cross-grain belt scratches are deep and extremely difficult to remove, and they show through any finish. The one exception is deliberate cross-grain levelling on very uneven stock, which then requires full re-sanding with the grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never clamp the work with the sander running near the clamp.</strong> The belt catching a clamp head jerks the machine violently. Use bench dogs, a stop block, or a non-slip mat so the workpiece is held without anything protruding into the sanding path.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Finish with a random orbit sander.</strong> The belt leaves directional scratches that a finish will highlight. Two grits with a random orbit machine afterwards removes them and takes a fraction of the time the belt work did.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pencil Lines Tell You When to Stop</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Before flattening a panel, scribble pencil lines across the whole surface. Sand until every line has disappeared and the surface is level by definition, because the low spots keep their pencil until the high spots have come down to meet them. It costs ten seconds and it is the single most useful trick in belt sanding.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Choosing and Looking After Belts -->
        <div id="belts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing and Looking After Belts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The belt matters at least as much as the machine, and belts are where most people economise to their cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start coarse and step through the grits.</strong> Sixty or eighty grit for flattening and stripping, then one hundred and twenty to remove those scratches. Skipping grits leaves deep scratches that the finer belt cannot reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Zirconia and ceramic belts outlast aluminium oxide.</strong> They cost more per belt and considerably less per hour of work, because they self-sharpen as they wear rather than simply going dull. For hardwood and stripping they are worth the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Watch the direction arrow.</strong> Most belts are joined with a lap splice that must run in the direction of travel or it catches and eventually separates. The arrow is printed on the inside for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean belts with a rubber stick.</strong> A crepe rubber cleaning stick held against a running belt pulls out the loaded resin and finish, and restores much of the cut. A belt that seems worn is very often just clogged.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lower the speed on finishes and resinous wood.</strong> Heat is what melts finish into the abrasive and burns pitch onto the belt. Running slower keeps the belt cutting rather than glazing, which is the main practical use of variable speed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store belts hanging or flat, not creased.</strong> A crease in the backing becomes a bump that thumps against the workpiece every revolution and eventually cracks. Hanging them over a dowel costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Dust Collection and Health -->
        <div id="dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dust Collection and Health
          </h2>

          <p>
            Belt sanders generate more dust per minute than any other handheld sander, and wood dust is a genuine health issue rather than merely a mess.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The bag is the minimum, not the answer.</strong> Onboard dust bags capture the coarse fraction and let the fine dust through. In our weighing tests, connecting a shop vacuum captured substantially more than any bag did.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fine dust is the dangerous fraction.</strong> The visible sawdust settles quickly; the invisible fine particulate stays airborne for hours and is what reaches deep into the lungs. Hardwood dust in particular is classified as a carcinogen in several jurisdictions.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a vacuum with a fine filter.</strong> An ordinary shop vacuum without a fine filter collects the dust and then blows the finest fraction straight back into the room, which is worse than useless. A HEPA or fine dust filter is the part that matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear a proper respirator, not a nuisance mask.</strong> A rated particulate respirator that seals to the face is a different thing from a paper dust mask. For any extended sanding session it is the single most worthwhile safety purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Old paint may contain lead.</strong> Sanding painted surfaces in a house built before the late 1970s can release lead dust, and a belt sander produces a great deal of it very quickly. Test first, and if it is positive, this is not a job for a belt sander.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sand outside or ventilate properly.</strong> Even with good collection, belt sanding indoors loads the air. Working outdoors or with cross ventilation and a running air cleaner keeps exposure down for the rest of the day as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: When Another Sander Is the Right Tool -->
        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Another Sander Is the Right Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying a belt sander for a job that wanted a different machine is common, and the distinctions are clear once stated.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Random orbit sanders are for finishing.</strong> The overlapping random pattern leaves no directional scratch, which is what allows a surface to take a finish evenly. They remove material slowly, which is a feature rather than a limitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Orbital sheet sanders are for light work and detail.</strong> Cheap, gentle, and useful for small areas and between coats. They will not flatten anything and they are slow, but they are difficult to damage a workpiece with.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drum and floor sanders are for floors.</strong> Using a handheld belt sander on a floor is possible and produces a wavy result over any real area, because the platen is too short to reference across the boards. Rent the right machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand planes still beat sanders for flattening.</strong> A sharp plane removes material faster than a belt sander on many jobs, leaves a better surface, makes no dust, and costs nothing to run. It requires skill that a sander does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bench mounting turns it into a stationary sander.</strong> Most belt sanders can be clamped upside down in a vice or fitted into a purpose made stand, at which point you bring the work to the machine instead. For shaping small parts, truing an end grain edge, or sharpening a scraper, that is far more controllable than trying to hold a small piece steady on a bench.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>An oscillating spindle sander handles curves.</strong> Belt sanders are flat-surface tools and cannot follow an inside curve at all. If your work involves shaped edges, templates or cut-outs, a spindle sander is the machine that does that job and no amount of technique makes a belt sander a substitute.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Own a belt sander and a random orbit sander.</strong> That pairing covers flattening and finishing, which is the great majority of workshop sanding. Everything else is a refinement on top of those two machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best belt sander?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Makita 9403 for benchtop and flattening work, thanks to a genuinely flat platen, stable tracking and unusually low noise. For a first belt sander in the more manageable 3 by 21 size, the Makita 9903 for its automatic tracking or the Porter-Cable 352VS for value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three by twenty-one inches suits most workshops, balancing coverage against controllability with belts available everywhere. Four by twenty-four removes material considerably faster on large flat surfaces but is heavy and much less forgiving. Compact machines are specialists for confined spaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use it for finishing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A belt runs in one direction and leaves directional scratches that show clearly under any finish. Use the belt sander to get the surface flat, then move to a random orbit sander for two grits to remove those scratches before finishing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does it gouge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always because the machine was stationary while running, or was tilted so pressure concentrated on one edge. Start the sander off the work, lower it while already moving forward, keep it flat and moving at all times, and lift it off while still moving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I sand a floor with one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over a small patch, yes. Over a room, no. The platen is too short to reference across boards, so it follows the existing contour and leaves a wavy surface that shows badly under finish. Rent a proper drum or orbital floor sander for anything beyond a repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grit should I start with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sixty or eighty grit for flattening and paint stripping, then step to one hundred and twenty to remove those scratches before moving to a random orbit sander. Skipping grits leaves deep scratches the finer belt cannot reach, which then show up once finish goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Belt Sander Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Belt sanders punish inattention faster than any other sander, and the errors are consistent enough to list.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting It Down While Running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stationary belt cuts a hollow in under two seconds, and correcting that hollow means taking the entire surface down to meet it. The machine should be moving before it touches the work and still moving when it leaves. This single habit prevents most belt sander damage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing Down Hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The weight of the machine is calibrated to the job. Adding pressure does not increase removal proportionally; it generates heat, clogs the belt with melted resin and finish, and makes the tool much harder to steer accurately. Guide it and let it work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding Across the Grain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cross-grain scratches from a belt sander are deep and extremely difficult to remove, and they show through any finish as a series of hard lines. Unless you are deliberately levelling very uneven stock and plan to re-sand fully with the grain, keep every pass aligned.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the Dust Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Onboard bags capture the coarse fraction and let the fine dust through, and the fine fraction is the one that stays airborne and reaches your lungs. Connect a shop vacuum with a fine filter, wear a rated respirator, and test old paint for lead before sanding it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

